#: includes/Admin/Screen/DisplaySetsPage.php:76
msgid "A display set is a named answer to \"what should this listing show\": which columns, in what order, which rooms and trainers, how far ahead, what to say when there is nothing. Pages refer to the set by name, so changing the set changes every page that uses it — without opening the page builder, and without touching the design."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Screen/DisplaySetsPage.php:274
msgid "A hall rented to somebody else occupies the room but is not a course of yours. On a full timetable of hall occupancy that matters; on the home page it usually does not."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Screen/MakeupPage.php:103
msgid "A make-up lesson replaces a class in exactly one course, but nothing in the timetable says which: the same name is used for the make-up slot of whichever course needs one, so two lessons named alike can belong to two different courses. Choose the course for each occurrence. Occurrences left unassigned are still counted and stored — they simply appear at no course. Nothing here is final: an assignment can be undone in the Assigned list."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Screen/MakeupPage.php:182
msgid "A make-up slot usually repeats: the same name, the same weekday, the same hour, the same room. Where you have already assigned one of a series, the others can be assigned like it in one press, and each row offers the same button on its own. Where two occurrences of one series were assigned to two different courses, nothing is offered — the series is not a series. The trainer is not part of the comparison, because the timetable fills it in some weeks and leaves it blank others; it is on screen for you to weigh."
msgstr ""

